		<div class="post" id="post-2575830">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2575830)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2575830">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mf99k" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2184508); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2184508&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056393" alt="Mf99k" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2184508)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mf99k" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2184508); return false;" >Mf99k</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472195867 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Aug 2016 07:17</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2575830">
									

<p>I've noticed many of the mistakes outlined here in many first drafts from new members, and I agree wholeheartedly with most of those mentioned here. However, as I have worked in many jobs that require varied forms of confidentiality, names, dates, and places ARE very often redacted, blackboxed or otherwise omitted. Though it may detract from the article if it's used too often, I usually find it necessary with names to use occasional redaction, for the person-in-mention's privacy (especially if some part of the story is based upon actual events). Unless I am using an explicit pseudonym, which I feel might attribute something to the wrong person in real life, the places I have worked for could get sued for using real names or not redacting confidential information in certain reports.</p>
<p>It's for sure a case-by-case basis, but certain details can detract from the overall narrative. The specific date and time something occurred, for example, isn't particularly important unless it directly relates to a specific timeline of events. And often these can be logged chronologically without specific information.</p>

							</div>

								
					
