				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=617958&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056393" alt="Communism will win" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=617958)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/communism-will-win" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(617958); return false;" >Communism will win</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472197883 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Aug 2016 07:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2575836">
									

<p>It's a nice thought, but in my experience, a lot of these errors are usually committed by people whose familiarity with the site is obviously very limited, i.e. they likely haven't ventured far out of the Top Rated Pages, much less into the various writing essays. I doubt many will read this and take the lessons to heart.</p>
<p>When all you have is the ability to write good essays, every new user looks like someone in need of an essay. All of this information is already present in existing SCPs that new users should be reading anyways. What they lack usually isn't a guide, but analytical reading skills, patience, command over the English language&#8230; stuff for their parents and teachers and growing up to teach them, not essays.</p>
<p>Careful and serious reading of SCPs is always going to be a better introduction to writing good SCPs than our prepackaged assessments of what makes good (or bad) SCPs. If we write essays, they should be on things you've learned from years of reflecting on articles, not things that any new user with something to prove can dump onto a coldpost's discussion page.</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>if your reading this your gay</p>

