				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056393" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472220536 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Aug 2016 14:08</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2576389">
									

<p>I'll vouch for essays like this being helpful- like I suspect at least a few others do, I started reading at series I and worked my way one by one toward series III.</p>
<p>I started brainstorming somewhere around SCP-200, and writing a draft at around SCP-1300.</p>
<p>Thus, while I had read a huge number of &quot;highly rated&quot; SCPs, I came very close to my first SCP attempt consisting largely of bullet points from the List of Cliches&#8230; before discovering that one and getting a wake-up call about the shift in site standards. This is essentially a less snarky, updated, and more constructive version of that one, so I'm glad to see it around to hopefully help others in the same way.</p>
<p>TL,DR: There are actually people who read these things and benefit greatly from them.</p>

							</div>

								
