				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2000367&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606056393" alt="WrongJohnSilver" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2000367)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;" >WrongJohnSilver</a></span> <span class="odate time_1472225769 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Aug 2016 15:36</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2576434">
									

<p>The big thing about &quot;rage state&quot; for me is that it reduces a complex emotional condition into a toggle. Ideas like a &quot;rage state&quot; are appropriate for a videogame mob, but not for a character in a story.</p>
<p>As much as some new authors might want to create new videogame characters to fight, this is not the place for them. Unless you're doing something like <a href="/scp-2424">SCP-2424</a>.</p>

							</div>

								
					
